 If you want to build swyh-rs yourself, you can find some information in the [wiki](https://github.com/dheijl/swyh-rs/wiki).
 
+#### macOS
+
+swyh-rs builds and runs on macOS (Apple Silicon and Intel), but there are two macOS specifics.
+
+**Build prerequisites.** The FLTK GUI is compiled from source by `fltk-sys`, which needs CMake and (because the `use-ninja` feature is enabled) Ninja:
+
+```sh
+brew install cmake ninja
+cargo build --release
+```
+
+The CLI binary needs neither: `cargo build --release --no-default-features --features cli`.
+
+**Capturing system audio.** CoreAudio has no loopback: unlike WASAPI on Windows or a PulseAudio/PipeWire monitor source on Linux, a macOS *output* device cannot be opened for capture. You need a virtual audio driver, for example [BlackHole](https://existential.audio/blackhole/) (free) or [Loopback](https://rogueamoeba.com/loopback/):
+
+1. Install the driver, e.g. `brew install blackhole-2ch`.
+2. In *Audio MIDI Setup*, create a **Multi-Output Device** combining your speakers and BlackHole, so you keep hearing the audio while it is being captured.
+3. Select that Multi-Output Device as the macOS sound output.
+4. Start swyh-rs and pick **BlackHole 2ch** as the audio source (it is auto-selected on first run when a loopback driver is detected).
+
+The first capture triggers the macOS microphone permission prompt; grant it to the app (or to Terminal, when running the binary from a terminal) under *System Settings → Privacy & Security → Microphone*. If the permission is denied, capture yields silence.
+
+If you select a plain output device such as "MacBook Air Speakers", swyh-rs logs an error saying the device cannot be opened for capture — that is this limitation, not a bug.
+
 If it doesn't work for you, please open a new issue and include all the debug log level information. I will try to provide a fix ASAP.
 
